  "step_3": "The decision reason specifically focuses on the outcome with the minimum probability in each lottery (i.e., the least probable outcome) and compares their monetary values. In this case, both lotteries have only one positive outcome, so the 'least probable outcome' corresponds to the positive outcome in each (2400 at 34% for A, 2500 at 33% for B). The individual considers essentially the same outcomes — the positive payoffs — and compares their probabilities and amounts. While the individual does not use the technical language of 'least probable outcome,' the content of the deliberation aligns with comparing the rarest positive events in each lottery and favoring the one with the higher payoff despite a slightly lower chance. The reasoning pattern matches the decision reason’s rule: prefer the lottery with the more favorable (higher-value) least probable outcome. Although the conceptual framing is not explicit, the informational basis and the comparative logic are consistent with the decision reason. Therefore, it is highly likely the individual used this reason, albeit implicitly.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
